Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether the extension $W$ (associated to $f = \tilde{p}(e)$) is a quotient of $V$ (associated to $e$), given a quotient $p: F \to Q$.

**Answer: Yes, $W$ is a quotient of $V$.**

**Proof.**

**Step 1: Identify $W$ as the pushout of $V$ along $p$.**

On a smooth curve $X$, we have the identification $H^1(\mathcal{G}) \cong \operatorname{Ext}^1(\mathcal{O}_X, \mathcal{G})$ for any coherent sheaf $\mathcal{G}$, via the local-to-global Ext spectral sequence (since $\operatorname{Hom}(\mathcal{O}_X, \mathcal{G}) = H^0(\mathcal{G})$ and $\operatorname{Ext}^2 = 0$ on a curve). Under this identification, the map $\tilde{p}: H^1(F) \to H^1(Q)$ corresponds to the pushforward $p_*: \operatorname{Ext}^1(\mathcal{O}_X, F) \to \operatorname{Ext}^1(\mathcal{O}_X, Q)$.

The pushforward of an extension class along $p: F \to Q$ is precisely the **pushout** construction. Concretely, the pushout of $0 \to F \xrightarrow{i} V \to \mathcal{O}_X \to 0$ along $p$ is
$$W = (V \oplus Q)\,/\,\{(i(a),\, -p(a)) : a \in F\},$$
and its extension class is $p_*(e) = \tilde{p}(e) = f$. Hence $W$ is exactly this pushout.

**Step 2: Construct the natural map $\pi: V \to W$.**

The pushout comes with a natural map $\pi: V \to W$ defined by $v \mapsto [(v, 0)]$, and a map $j: Q \to W$ defined by $q \mapsto [(0, q)]$. These fit into a morphism of short exact sequences:

$$\begin{array}{ccccccccc}
0 & \to & F & \xrightarrow{i} & V & \to & \mathcal{O}_X & \to & 0 \\
& & \downarrow{p} & & \downarrow{\pi} & & \downarrow{\mathrm{id}} \\
0 & \to & Q & \xrightarrow{j} & W & \to & \mathcal{O}_X & \to & 0
\end{array}$$

The left square commutes: $\pi \circ i(a) = [(i(a), 0)] = [(0, p(a))] = j \circ p(a)$, since $(i(a), 0) - (0, p(a)) = (i(a), -p(a))$ lies in the relation subgroup. The right square commutes by construction of the pushout (the map $V \to \mathcal{O}_X$ factors through $W$).

**Step 3: Apply the snake lemma to show $\pi$ is surjective.**

Apply the snake lemma to the morphism of short exact sequences above, with $\alpha = p$, $\beta = \pi$, $\gamma = \mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{O}_X}$. The snake lemma gives the exact sequence:

$$0 \to \ker p \to \ker \pi \to \ker(\mathrm{id}) \xrightarrow{\delta} \operatorname{coker} p \to \operatorname{coker} \pi \to \operatorname{coker}(\mathrm{id}) \to 0.$$

Now:
- $p$ is surjective (it is a quotient map), so $\operatorname{coker} p = 0$.
- $\mathrm{id}_{\mathcal{O}_X}$ is an isomorphism, so $\ker(\mathrm{id}) = 0$ and $\operatorname{coker}(\mathrm{id}) = 0$.

Substituting, the exact sequence becomes:

$$0 \to \ker p \to \ker \pi \to 0 \xrightarrow{\delta} 0 \to \operatorname{coker} \pi \to 0 \to 0.$$

From exactness, $\operatorname{coker} \pi = 0$, i.e., **$\pi: V \to W$ is surjective**. (Additionally, $\ker \pi \cong \ker p = K$, which is consistent: the kernel of the quotient map $V \to W$ is the subbundle $K \hookrightarrow F \hookrightarrow V$.)

**Conclusion.**

The surjection $\pi: V \twoheadrightarrow W$ exhibits $W$ as a quotient of $V$. The kernel of this quotient is precisely $K = \ker(p)$, embedded into $V$ via $F \hookrightarrow V$. In other words, $W \cong V / K$, and the extension $0 \to Q \to W \to \mathcal{O}_X \to 0$ is obtained from $0 \to F \to V \to \mathcal{O}_X \to 0$ by modding out the subbundle $K = \ker(p)$ on the left.

